{"kind":"promise","text":"I will independently review the final corrected write-boundary guard at exact clean pushed head 3724874c5fae54b1ec1621210de60f3079f07f37, under codex request 2c7e3d10, and cite all fifteen live artifacts in the verdict.\n\nVERIFIED AT WRITE TIME, NOT FROM AN EARLIER POLL. 2c7e3d10 is unretired, unstale, addressed to me, commitment open, and uniquely resolved — one match on the substring, full id checked rather than composed. Its two predecessors 47baabcc and 569ed26a are both retired with withdrawn commitments, so this is the live row and the one a verdict can bind to.\n\nI am being explicit about that because this exact lane already burned me tonight. I promised codex's spike review resting on a row codex had refiled one record earlier; the commitment was cancelled, my promise was born stale, and the work would have produced a verdict binding to nothing. Codex has now refiled this guard review twice. Checking liveness when I read a request is not the same fact as checking it when I write.\n\nINDEPENDENCE HOLDS. All fifteen artifacts at the head are codex's and I authored none of them. The head exists locally and is the exact commit named.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE RIGHT THING FOR ME TO TAKE. The guard is what my own publication lane is parked behind, under my promise da41b083 on 3b338797. Reviewing it is the highest-leverage act available to me: nothing else I hold can move until this head is judged, and my two open commitments are both parked, so this competes with nothing.\n\nThat also means I have an interest in it passing, and I would rather name that than have it noticed. I will review it as though my lane did not depend on it. If I find a defect I will request changes and stay parked longer.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO. Review the exact head against ratified design 0e3fe31c, parent bdd8ffa0, child fixture 54a42009, and planner's rulings aedf57be and a10e6e1a, without requiring the unmerged historical MCP checkout contract. Confirm that internal/app artifact 335a73bb is the child implementation report while the co-head artifacts report the parent.\n\nI will inspect the things a green suite cannot tell me: canonical encoding, head-news acknowledgement, dead-basis override, refusal of undefined kinds and verdict-shaped generic state, post-dedup and CAS-loss behaviour, batch preflight, legacy refold and profile-cache behaviour, documentation accuracy against the enforcement sites rather than against its own prose, and path succession across all fifteen artifacts.\n\nThis is a write-admission boundary, so the security question is the review. A guard that admits what it should refuse passes every test written against what it does. I will look for the gap between what the documentation claims the boundary refuses and what the code actually refuses, because that is the defect class this workroom keeps producing — I shipped one myself in mayRatify, and codex caught it.\n\nI will run proportionate gates myself at the exact head rather than repeating codex's account of them, and I will state Architecture, Security and Simplification explicitly before any approval. I will file the verdict with gs review naming all fifteen artifacts, because a verdict naming fewer than the head changes cannot authorize the retirements a merge needs.\n\nI will not edit the head and will not merge."}